import{_ as a,P as i,d as n,e as o,x as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.13 Kinship, Exchange, Ritual, Ethnography, and Cultural Change","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.13 Kinship, Exchange, Ritual, Ethnography, and Cultural Change","xkChars":0,"xkWords":479,"xkLessons":{"D.13":{"title":"D.13 Kinship, exchange, ritual, ethnography, and cultural change","lead":"Participating in a ritual together can establish shared attendance; shared belief, accurate memory, and future cooperation each need their own material. Ethnography can supplement experiences omitted by tables, but must also state the boundaries of entry, recording, disclosure, and representation.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4809–4824; source anchor section-d-13; short quotation: ‘The existence of rituals, physiological synchrony, expressions of identity, accuracy of memory, and sustained cooperation each have their own evidence units’ (translated from Chinese). The source slice for this topic is attached verbatim in source-slice.md, keeping the explanation separate from the source.","Kinship rules, gift debts, ritual classifications, habitus and capital, ecological livelihoods, and ethnography have their respective units of analysis. Kinship terms do not directly equal actual care networks; nonmarket exchange does not mean the absence of obligations; and gifts and commodities may intertwine within one relationship. Judge particular exchange arrangements, expectations, and power, rather than declaring a relationship purely a gift or purely a market exchange simply from whether money is used.","Ritual coordination, public identity, private belief, and long-term cooperation may not move together. Members may attend together out of identification, etiquette, role requirements, income, or avoidance of conflict; synchronized movements do not directly establish identical inner beliefs either. One visible behavior cannot fill in other invisible outcomes, and a ritual's complete performance cannot replace checks of subsequent cooperation and distribution.","Symbolic interpretation asks how practices are understood in a specific context, while material and institutional studies trace resources, labor, constraints, and consequences. They can correct each other: a resource arrangement may change a ritual's meaning, and meaning may in turn influence action. But a detailed interpretation of meaning does not automatically establish resource causation, nor does a set of input-output numbers exhaust participants' understanding. Mutual checking does not mean discharging each other's evidentiary responsibilities.","Habitus refers to stable dispositions that may form through long-term practice, not an entirely fixed essence of personality. Multiple socializations, conflicts between fields, and environmental changes may revise dispositions. Record how practices form, under which conditions they stabilize, and when they change, rather than inferring from one action or one class label that a whole life must follow the same path.","Conversion of capital depends on recognition and rules within a particular field. A skill, title, or relationship securing opportunities in one setting need not retain the same effect elsewhere. Conversion is not unconditional energy conservation, and all resources cannot be summed on an unexplained common scale. If an original author makes a stronger claim to an overall explanation, retain that strong commitment for evaluation rather than first weakening it into common sense and then declaring complete compatibility.","Entry, trust, recording, disclosure, and representative authority in ethnography are not different names for one permission. Permission to enter a setting does not automatically permit recording all private content; willingness to be interviewed need not imply consent to unlimited publication; and a researcher's familiarity with a setting does not mean knowledge of every member's wishes. Each kind of standing requires its own grounds. In particular, the absence of people not contacted or unable to speak safely cannot be treated as consent.","The detail of thick description can reveal contexts omitted by standard survey forms, but an observer's presence may also change expression. Explain the route of entry, who was willing to speak, who was not contacted, who could not speak safely, and how the researcher affected interactions. Reflexivity does not invalidate all material by calling it subjective; it incorporates how the material was formed into the explanation so readers can understand its scope and omissions.","Cultural change need not be understood solely as a choice between authentic and fabricated tradition. The source's festival example involving new media allows both continued creativity in a craft and the disappearance of certain experiences, recording both together. A new expression need not therefore be false, and continuity of tradition does not establish that internal control and distribution are unproblematic. Who has authority to decide how change occurs, who benefits, and who bears losses still require separate discussion.","Disagreements between cultural relativism and common protections must be addressed through specific reasons. The words 'respect for culture' cannot cancel protection for affected people, nor can an external scale alone establish that all local meanings are worthless. Explaining why a practice matters to people and deciding whether a particular harm is permissible are different tasks; positions of disagreement and criticism within the culture must remain visible.","Do not first rewrite an original author's strong commitments as neutral reminders. If the source claims a definite order of priority, a unified scale, or an overall coordinating role for symbols, material conditions, or conversions of capital, present that claim accurately and ask what materials could support or refute it. Using a useful part of an analysis and accepting its entire theory are different decisions. Compatibility must have boundaries; it cannot be obtained by deleting the disagreements.","The existence of a ritual, physiological synchrony such as heart-rate synchrony, expressions of identity, accurate memory, and lasting cooperation each have their own units of evidence. Several papers sharing the same event or underlying sample may analyze different outcomes, but the number of papers is not the number of independent validations; repeated analysis cannot be passed off as new sampling. The eight entry points, in order, are kinship and gifts in Mauss, Lévi-Strauss, and others; ritual and communal feeling in Durkheim, van Gennep, and Turner; symbolic classification in Geertz and Douglas; habitus, field, and capital in Bourdieu; cultural materialism, ecology, and subsistence; ethnographic thick description and participant observation; relativism and common protections; and hybridity, revival, and change. This directory cannot replace the complete original texts of these theories with their strong commitments."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Joint attendance","leftText":"Being present at the same activity.","rightTitle":"Belief, memory, and cooperation","rightText":"Private beliefs, accurate memory, and subsequent behavior each require additional materials."},{"leftTitle":"Interpretation of symbolic meaning","leftText":"Describes how a practice is understood in context.","rightTitle":"Material and institutional mechanisms","rightText":"Trace resource constraints and consequences; interpreting meaning cannot substitute for this."},{"leftTitle":"Access to a field and trust","leftText":"Establishing a particular relationship of contact and communication.","rightTitle":"Recording, disclosure, and authority to represent","rightText":"Each use needs its own grounds and boundaries."},{"leftTitle":"Continuing cultural creativity","leftText":"An older practice produces new expressions under new conditions.","rightTitle":"Continuity without loss","rightText":"New creation can coexist with losses and internal disagreement."},{"leftTitle":"Multiple research papers","leftText":"May present different outcomes or analyses.","rightTitle":"Multiple independent validations","rightText":"Require checking events, samples, and information dependence; they cannot simply be counted by paper."}],"boundaries":["Do not equate familiarity with a field with authorization from, or representation of, all its members; do not disclose ethnographic materials without permission.","Keep joint attendance, physiological synchrony, expressions of identity, memory, and cooperation separate; evidence for one does not fill the gaps in the others.","Do not automatically describe cultural change as fabrication, or treat the continuation of tradition as proof that internal control and harm are absent.","Do not erase an original author's strong theoretical commitments to manufacture compatibility.","This lesson involved no fieldwork or participation in dangerous rituals. All everyday scenarios are teaching constructions; the original research was not reanalyzed."],"questions":["Why is thick description valuable while also needing to account for observer effects?","Why can capital not be converted unconditionally?","How should three papers sharing an underlying sample be counted?","Why does using part of a theory not establish compatibility with the whole?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-13/figures/section-d-13.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual primary research: heart-rate recordings during a fire-walking ritual","href":"https://liology.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2011/05/konvalinka-synchronized-arousal.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual primary research: heart-rate recordings during a fire-walking ritual","text":"The 2011 paper by Konvalinka and colleagues recorded the heart rates of 12 fire-walkers, 9 related spectators, and 17 unrelated spectators in San Pedro Manrique, Spain: 38 people in total. The first Results paragraph and the methods describe recordings averaged over 5-second intervals and analysis of ritual and baseline periods. The paper discusses shared arousal and explicitly states on page 5 of the Early Edition that it remains unclear whether this is a driving mechanism or an emergent property. Such heart-rate evidence must not directly stand in for lasting cooperation or accurate memory among all participants.","sourceLabel":"Konvalinka et al., 2011, doi:10.1073/pnas.1016955108; publicly available copy of the original paper's Early Edition","sourceUrl":"https://liology.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2011/05/konvalinka-synchronized-arousal.pdf","sourceLocator":"PNAS Early Edition, page 2, first Results paragraph; page 5, Discussion paragraph beginning 'It remains unclear'; Participants and the first two Materials and Procedure paragraphs in Materials and Methods","boundary":"The methods and the authors' uncertainty were checked against the original passages read. RQA/CRQA was not re-estimated, no values were read from figures, and the observational study is not described as a randomized trial. The PDF screenshot interface failed, so no judgments were based on images.","href":"https://liology.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2011/05/konvalinka-synchronized-arousal.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.13","start_line":4900,"end_line":4915,"startLine":4900,"endLine":4915}],"outputId":"section-d-13","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Fix the units of analysis for kinship, exchange, ritual, symbols, resources, and related matters, so that one visible behavior does not fill in every relational outcome.","Check contextual meaning against material and institutional consequences, retaining the domain conditions governing changes in habitus and conversions of capital.","Check access, trust, recording, disclosure, and authority to represent separately. State observer effects, who was not reached, and circumstances in which speaking is unsafe.","Record continuing cultural creativity and specific losses together, and discuss internal control, distribution, and common protections.","Retain original authors' strong commitments as stated in their texts. Trace the underlying evidence sample for each outcome, without inflating independent validation by counting papers."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Developing the source's teaching example: preservation and loss through new media","text":"Suppose a festival introduces video distribution: participation from elsewhere and some revenue increase, and a craft is learned and passed on, while the former experience of meeting face to face diminishes. New creation, continuity of the craft, and specific losses can each be recognized. A new medium alone does not make the expression fabricated, and preserving a craft does not establish fairness in income distribution or decision-making authority.","assumptions":"Developed from the source; no actual festival was investigated.","boundary":"Every real-world effect requires its own materials. The conditions in this example are not ready-made proof for any cultural policy."},{"title":"Teaching construction: attendance does not establish shared belief","text":"Suppose three people attend the same ritual: one endorses its meaning, one observes a family courtesy, and one attends because of work requirements. Their attendance records are the same while their motives differ. Research on subsequent cooperation needs additional observation; a seating list cannot directly supply shared belief and stable solidarity.","assumptions":"Motives are specified by the teaching scenario; real motives cannot be read directly from someone's mind.","boundary":"This does not deny the limited coordination or identity significance of attendance; it rejects only inferences beyond those bounds."},{"title":"Teaching construction: converting capital between two fields","text":"Suppose someone holds a skills certificate recognized in industry A that does not meet eligibility requirements in industry B. The certificate still exists, but whether it can be converted into opportunities depends on B's rules and recognition. A count of certificates cannot be treated as a quantity of energy convertible at an equivalent rate across all fields.","assumptions":"The industry certificates and rules are fictional.","boundary":"This does not adjudicate actual professional qualifications or employment opportunities, or summarize Bourdieu's entire theory."},{"title":"Teaching construction: three papers still draw on one event","text":"Suppose data from the same ritual and the same 30 participants are used in three papers on physiology, interviews, and memory. More kinds of outcome may increase understanding, but there is still 1 independent event and still 30 participants counted without duplication. This must not be described as 90 people or three independent replications.","assumptions":"The data size and the three papers are constructions by the lesson author; no actual study is accused of duplicate publication.","boundary":"Sharing an underlying sample does not mean there is no added value; dependence and outcome units simply need to be identified accurately."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Everyone attended together, so they must privately agree, remember accurately, and cooperate over the long term.","effect":"Visible joint attendance cannot supply the missing evidence about private beliefs, memory, and behavioral outcomes.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"New media change a tradition, so the new expression must be false.","effect":"Change may be real continuing creativity, while losses and distributional issues still need to be recorded.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"First replace a theory's order of priority with 'all factors matter,' then claim that all schools are fully compatible.","effect":"This discards the original author's strong commitments. Retain them as stated in the source before assessing limited use.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why is thick description valuable while also needing to account for observer effects?","answer":"It can reveal experiences omitted by a table, but access and interaction shape how the materials are produced. Those boundaries should be transparent rather than grounds for rejecting it wholesale.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can capital not be converted unconditionally?","answer":"Its effectiveness depends on recognition and rules within a field. Across fields, it may cease to work or require additional conditions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How should three papers sharing an underlying sample be counted?","answer":"Register the three analyses or outcomes separately while retaining their dependence on the same underlying sample; do not describe them as three independent replications.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does using part of a theory not establish compatibility with the whole?","answer":"The original theory may make stronger commitments about priority and unified explanation. Using a local tool cannot settle the case for its entire argument.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-13/figures/section-d-13.png","alt":"Kinship, exchange, and ritual each have their own units of analysis; gifts can also impose obligations. Check meaning against resource consequences; neither pays in place of the other. Check the grounds for access, trust, recording, disclosure, and representation individually. Continuing cultural creativity can coexist with loss; control and distribution require separate investigation. Attendance, physiological synchrony, identity, memory, and cooperation each have their own units of evidence. Retain strong theoretical commitments; multiple papers sharing an underlying sample do not count as multiple independent validations. Drawn by the lesson author from D.13; the teaching examples are not actual fieldwork. The figure neither copies images from the original paper nor presents raw data.","caption":"Drawn by the lesson author from D.13; the teaching examples are not actual fieldwork. The figure neither copies images from the original paper nor presents raw data."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a proposal for later design. Switching material types and sample dependencies would show which independent support is needed for attendance, physiology, beliefs, memory, and cooperation.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original methods delimit the units of physiological evidence and retain the authors' unresolved mechanism question; ritual research is not used to leap to private beliefs, accurate memory, or lasting cooperation."},"openQuestions":["The complete original theoretical texts for 13.01–13.08 were not obtained; the directory does not replace a full assessment of their strong commitments.","The case checks only the explicitly listed PDF passages on methods and discussion. Raw heart-rate data, supplementary materials, and independent replications were not rechecked; PDF screenshots were not obtained."],"taskId":"S-D-13","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research draft, full-text lines 4721–4736; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["D.13: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"konvalinka2011","title":"Synchronized arousal between performers and related spectators in a fire-walking ritual","url":"https://liology.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2011/05/konvalinka-synchronized-arousal.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2011; PNAS DOI10.1073/pnas.1016955108, six-page Early Edition","locator":"Early Edition, p2, first Results paragraph; p5, Discussion paragraph beginning 'It remains unclear'; p5, Participants and the first two Materials and Procedure paragraphs in Materials and Methods","claim_support":["Heart-rate recording groups of 12+9+17=38, 5-second averaging, and baseline and ritual periods; the authors leave unresolved whether the phenomenon is a mechanism or an emergent property"],"limits":["Only the specified methods and discussion passages were read. Data and figures were not re-estimated, and the PDF screenshot interface failed. Heart rate is not used to decide claims about lasting cooperation or memory."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.13 Kinship, Exchange, Ritual, Ethnography, and Cultural Change; Chinese source lines 4809–4824; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-13/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-13/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-13/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-13/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"f15d281ad063d15f20e3dca5c9c9696f06e2e935efd92548440980bf5fafc588","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-13.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-13.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-13.md"};function d(h,e,u,p,m,f){const t=i("SectionLesson");return n(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s("",1)),r(t,{section:"D.13"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=a(l,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
